WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.195
The CPU supports tokenization, application logic, and inference when layers remain on the processor.

00:00:07.195 --> 00:00:11.604
Core behavior and memory bandwidth influence CPU performance.

00:00:11.604 --> 00:00:18.799
CPU-only systems can run useful smaller quantized models but may have lower generation speed.

00:00:18.799 --> 00:00:23.673
A discrete GPU provides parallel compute and dedicated VRAM.

00:00:23.673 --> 00:00:30.403
VRAM must hold model weights, cache, activations, and runtime overhead for accelerated work.

00:00:30.403 --> 00:00:37.134
The operating system and display may also consume part of the available memory.

00:00:37.134 --> 00:00:43.864
System RAM supports the operating system, applications, model loading, CPU-resident layers, and cache.

00:00:43.864 --> 00:00:48.738
Unified-memory systems share a pool between CPU and GPU.

00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:55.933
Shared capacity can increase flexibility, but bandwidth, thermals, and total memory still limit performance.

00:00:55.933 --> 00:01:01.271
Storage affects download capacity, startup, model switching, logs, and backups.

00:01:01.271 --> 00:01:06.609
Keeping several parameter sizes and quantizations can consume substantial space.

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:11.947
Fast storage cannot compensate for insufficient working memory during inference.

00:01:11.947 --> 00:01:15.428
Power and cooling determine sustained behavior.

00:01:15.428 --> 00:01:22.159
A laptop may benchmark well briefly and slow under heat or battery limits.

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:25.640
Desktops offer easier cooling and upgrades.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.514
Mini PCs trade compactness for expansion and thermal constraints.

00:01:30.514 --> 00:01:37.708
Apple Silicon, NVIDIA, AMD, and CPU-only systems use different acceleration paths and software support.

00:01:37.708 --> 00:01:46.760
Inventory exact hardware, available memory, operating system, drivers, power mode, and cooling before using someone else's performance claim.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:52.562
Use available memory, not the number printed on a product page.

00:01:52.562 --> 00:02:01.613
Displays, operating systems, background applications, security tools, and other services reduce the capacity the runtime can actually allocate.
